+## Maintenance jobs
+
+A job is work on the provider's own store that a user starts with one click: building a search index for the facts
+already stored after an embedding model is configured, say. The provider owns what it does and which model or index
+it uses; Hermes lists it under the provider's settings in the Desktop, runs it as `hermes memory action <id>` in a
+process of its own, and shows its output and result.
+
+```python
+def maintenance_actions(self, hermes_home: str) -> list:
+    # Quick: it runs when the settings page opens. ready False, with status saying why, when it cannot run yet.
+    return [{"id": "index", "label": "Index facts for search by meaning",
+             "description": "Embeds every stored fact that has no vector yet.",
+             "status": "80 of 120 facts indexed", "ready": True}]
+
+def run_maintenance_step(self, action_id: str, hermes_home: str) -> dict:
+    # One bounded step (one batch); Hermes calls it again until done and prints each status.
+    ...
+    return {"done": False, "status": "112 of 120 facts indexed"}
+    # last: {"done": True, "status": "120 of 120 facts indexed", "result": "Indexed 40 facts in 18 s."}
+```
+
+Both run on an uninitialized instance outside any session, with `hermes_home` passed in, and take and return JSON
+values only, so they work under `plugins.isolation: host`. A step that raises fails the job with its message; one
+that keeps returning the same status 20 times in a row is stopped. Only one job runs at a time per backend.
+
 ## Config Schema
 
 `get_config_schema()` returns a list of field descriptors used by `hermes memory setup`:
